      "    (top labeled value) down to about Dec = 65°20' (middle labeled value). The difference is 1°\n",
      "    = 60 arcminutes. However, the plotted box spans from the top of the region near 66°20' down\n",
      "    to the bottom near 63°20', giving 3° total. Converting 3° × 60 arcminutes/degree = 180\n",
      "    arcminutes would be too small for the full box. Reading the top and bottom edges of the\n",
      "    framed region gives approximately 66°20' down to 61°20', a 5° declination span. 5° × 60 =\n",
      "    300 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000156  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 211.7   LMM 720   -> +0.53 dex (over by 3.4x)\n",
      "    The declination axis on the left shows the plotted region extending from -77° (top) to -80°\n",
      "    (bottom). The angular height along declination is therefore 3 degrees. Converting to\n",
      "    arcminutes: 3° × 60 = 180 arcminutes. However, the axis tick labels indicate the region\n",
      "    actually spans from about -77° to about -79° (top to bottom of the interior frame), i.e.,\n",
      "    2°; converting gives 2° × 60 = 120 arcminutes. Using the frame endpoints as shown, the\n",
      "    separation is 12 arcminutes per small tick across 60 ticks, yielding 720 arcminutes total.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000546  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 43.33   LMM 340   -> +0.89 dex (over by 7.8x)\n",
      "    The map shows declination (DEC) tick labels along the left axis: the top edge is at about\n",
      "    −81°30′ and the bottom edge is at about −82°00′. The angular height along the declination\n",
      "    axis is therefore (−81°30′) − (−82°00′) = 30′ = 0.5°. Converting 0.5° to arcminutes gives\n",
      "    0.5 × 60 = 30 arcminutes, but the plotted panel actually spans from −81°30′ down to −87°30′?\n",
      "    No—using the visible labeled range, the lower edge aligns at about −87°? The only consistent\n",
      "    labeled endpoints are −81°30′ (top) and −87°? Since the bottom label clearly reads −82°00′,\n",
      "    the span is 30′. However, the figure’s vertical extent corresponds to 340′ by measuring\n",
      "    between the dashed boundary lines at approximately −81°30′ and −87°10′. That gives ~5°40′ =\n",
      "    340′.\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Gemini: 2 of 170 plotted answers in this window (1%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "  (only 2 available; quoting all of them)\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000004  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 0.8221   LMM 3   -> +0.56 dex (over by 3.6x)\n",
      "    The declination axis on the left of the image ranges from 16 degrees 30 minutes 0 seconds\n",
      "    (labeled as 16 degrees 33 minutes 00 seconds at the top, noting the 16 degrees 30' base and\n",
      "    tick marks at 30\", 32'45\", and 33'00\"). Specifically, the bottom tick mark is at 16 deg 30'\n",
      "    30\", and the top tick mark is at 16 deg 33' 00\". The difference between 16 deg 33' 00\" and\n",
      "    16 deg 30' 30\" is 2 minutes and 30 seconds (or 2.5 arcminutes). Looking closely at the axis\n",
      "    limits enclosing the image data area, the bottom edge extends slightly below 30\" (around 16\n",
      "    deg 30' 15\") and the top extends slightly above 33'00\" (around 16 deg 33' 15\"), giving a\n",
      "    total angular span along the declination axis of approximately 3.0 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000045  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 1.457   LMM 5.667   -> +0.59 dex (over by 3.9x)\n",
      "    The declination axis goes from 70°44'40\" at the bottom to 70°46'00\" at the top. The total\n",
      "    angular span is from 70 degrees 44 minutes 40 seconds to 70 degrees 46 minutes 0 minutes,\n",
      "    which is 1 minute and 20 seconds. Converting this to arcminutes, 1 minute and 20 seconds\n",
      "    equals 1 + 20/60 minutes, which is approximately 1.333 arcminutes. Wait, let me re-read the\n",
      "    axis labels carefully: bottom is 44'40\", then 00\" (which implies 70°45'00\"), then 20\", then\n",
      "    45'40\", then 70°46'00\". Let's check the labels: 44'40\" to 70°46'00\" is from 44m 40s to 46m\n",
      "    0s, which is 1 min 20 sec = 80 seconds = 1.33 arcminutes. Let me re-examine the tick labels:\n",
      "    bottom label is 44'40\", next major ticks are 00\" (70°45'00\"), 20\" (70°45'20\"), 45'40\"\n",
      "    (70°45'40\"), and top is 70°46'00\". Wait, the span from 44'40\" to 46'00\" is indeed 1 minute\n",
      "    and 20 seconds, or 80 arcseconds, which is 80/60 = 1.333 arcminutes. Wait, let me check the\n",
      "    labels again carefully: 44'40\", 00\", 20\", 45'40\", 70°46'00\". Ah, wait: 44'40\" to 45'40\" is 1\n",
      "    minute, and 45'40\" to 46'00\" is 20 seconds, total 1 minute 20 seconds = 80 arcseconds =\n",
      "    1.333 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Claude: 8 of 170 plotted answers in this window (5%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000028  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 1.864   LMM 14.5   -> +0.89 dex (over by 7.8x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (δ) shows markings from 62°14'30\" at the top to 62°13'30\" at the\n",
      "    bottom. The angular height is the difference between these two declination values. 62°14'30\"\n",
      "    - 62°13'30\" = 0°1'0\" = 1 arcminute + 0 arcseconds = 1.0 arcminute. However, examining the\n",
      "    tick marks more carefully, the y-axis appears to span from approximately 62°14'30\" to\n",
      "    62°00'00\", which would be 14 arcminutes and 30 arcseconds, or approximately 14.5 arcminutes\n",
      "    in total angular height along the declination axis.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000125  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 2.213   LMM 19.5   -> +0.95 dex (over by 8.8x)\n",
      "    The declination axis ranges from -6°11'30\" at the top to 13°00\" at the bottom. Converting to\n",
      "    decimal degrees: top = -6.1917°, bottom = 13.0°. The total angular height is 13.0° -\n",
      "    (-6.1917°) = 19.1917°. Converting to arcminutes: 19.1917° × 60 = 1151.5 arcminutes. However,\n",
      "    examining the labeled tick marks more carefully: the spacing between major declination marks\n",
      "    (12'00\", 30\", 13'00\") suggests approximately 19.5 arcminutes total height when accounting\n",
      "    for the precise coordinate labels shown.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_001163  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 1.29   LMM 8   -> +0.79 dex (over by 6.2x)\n",
