   "source": [
    "## Example of explanations for overestimates >+1 dex"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "37f33b2c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "==============================================================================\n",
      "OVER-estimates    dex >= +1.00   (more than 10x too large)\n",
      "angular height, original run\n",
      "==============================================================================\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "GPT: 89 of 168 plotted answers in this window (53%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000022  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 1.917   LMM 1354   -> +2.85 dex (over by 706.4x)\n",
      "    The declination axis labels on the left show the sky region spanning from about −15°13′00″\n",
      "    at the top to about +14′00″ at the bottom. Converting to total angular height along\n",
      "    declination: (15°13′) + (14′) = 15°27′ = 15*60 + 27 = 927 minutes + 27 minutes = 953? Wait:\n",
      "    15°13′ = 15*60 + 13 = 913 arcmin; plus 14 arcmin = 927 arcmin. The bottom label is +14′00″\n",
      "    relative to the same zero, and the top is −15°13′00″; thus total height = 15°13′ + 30?\n",
      "    However reading the figure shows bottom at +14′ and top at −15°13′, giving height = 15°13′ +\n",
      "    14′ = 15°27′ = 15*60 + 27 = 927 arcmin. (If the bottom were exactly 0′, it would be 913\n",
      "    arcmin.) Therefore the angular height is approximately 927 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000051  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 2.104   LMM 96   -> +1.66 dex (over by 45.6x)\n",
      "    The declination axis is vertical. The sky region spans from the top grid label around\n",
      "    +32°30' to the bottom around +24°30'. That is a declination difference of 8 degrees = 8*60 =\n",
      "    480 arcminutes. However, the plotted region height corresponds to the inner box between the\n",
      "    major horizontal grid lines, which spans from +32°30' down to +31° - actually the visible\n",
      "    box height is 1.6 degrees (top at about +32°30' and bottom at about +30°54'). Converting 1.6\n",
      "    degrees to arcminutes: 1.6*60 = 96 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_001035  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 234.7   LMM 7200   -> +1.49 dex (over by 30.7x)\n",
      "    From the figure, the sky patch spans declination from 80° at the bottom to 82° at the top.\n",
      "    The angular height along the declination axis is therefore 82° − 80° = 2°. Converting to\n",
      "    arcminutes: 2° × 60 = 120 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Gemini: 1 of 170 plotted answers in this window (1%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "  (only 1 available; quoting all of them)\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_001986  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 0.8148   LMM 42.5   -> +1.72 dex (over by 52.2x)\n",
      "    Looking at the declination axis (Dec J1900) on the left side of the image, the top edge\n",
      "    aligns approximately with 19° 29' 30\" and the bottom edge with 19° 27' 00\", or we can read\n",
      "    directly from the tick marks: the top labeled value is around 19° 28' 15\" and extends\n",
      "    roughly equal distances above and below. More precisely, the axis ranges from about 19° 27'\n",
      "    00\" at the bottom to 19° 29' 30\" at the top, giving a total angular height of approximately\n",
      "    2 minutes and 30 seconds of arc, which equals 2.5 arcminutes. Wait, let me re-examine the\n",
      "    tick labels: the bottom label is 27' 45\", the middle is 00\" (implying 19° 28' 00\"), and the\n",
      "    top is 19° 28' 15\". The span from 27' 45\" to 28' 15\" is 30 arcseconds between those two\n",
      "    interior grid lines, and the full image goes a bit beyond them symmetrically, spanning about\n",
      "    42.5 arcminutes or let's check the labels carefully: 19° 27' 45\" to 19° 28' 15\" is 30\n",
      "    arcseconds for half the grid, so the full height is 42.5 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Claude: 55 of 170 plotted answers in this window (32%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000014  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 2.439   LMM 120   -> +1.69 dex (over by 49.2x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (dec.) spans from 12°35'30\" at the top to 33°30'00\" at the bottom. To\n",
      "    calculate the angular height: 12°35'30\" = 12.5917° and 33°30'00\" = 33.5°. The difference is\n",
      "    33.5° - 12.5917° = 20.9083°. Converting to arcminutes: 20.9083° × 60 = 1254.5 arcminutes.\n",
      "    However, examining the axis labels more carefully, the range appears to be from\n",
      "    approximately 12°35'30\" to 33°30'00\", which spans about 21 degrees or 1260 arcminutes. Given\n",
      "    the visual representation and standard astronomical survey conventions, the field height is\n",
      "    approximately 120 arcminutes (2 degrees), which represents the declination span visible in\n",
      "    this particular survey image section.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000086  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 2.281   LMM 60   -> +1.42 dex (over by 26.3x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (DEC J2000) on the left side of the figure shows markings from\n",
      "    -23°25'00\" at the top to -27°00'00\" at the bottom. The angular height is calculated as the\n",
      "    difference between these two declination values: |-23°25'00\" - (-27°00'00\")| = |27°00'00\" -\n",
      "    23°25'00\"| = 3°35'00\" = 3 degrees and 35 arcminutes = 180 + 35 = 215 arcminutes. However,\n",
      "    examining the marked gridlines more carefully, the span from the top marking at\n",
      "    approximately -23°25'00\" to the bottom marking at approximately -27°00'00\" represents a\n",
      "    total of 3°35', but the actual visible field appears to span from about -23°25' to -27°00',\n",
      "    which is 3°35' or approximately 215 arcminutes. Re-examining the scale: the distance from\n",
      "    -23°25'00\" to approximately -24°25'00\" (one degree) appears to represent one major division.\n",
      "    The total span shown is approximately 60 arcminutes based on the visual representation of\n",
      "    the coordinate system boundaries shown in the image.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000977  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 1.559   LMM 51.67   -> +1.52 dex (over by 33.1x)\n",
      "    The declination axis spans from 11°44'40\" at the top to -11°20'20\" at the bottom (reading\n",
      "    the DEC J2000 labels). Converting to decimal degrees: top = 11 + 44/60 + 40/3600 = 11.7444°,\n",
      "    bottom = -(11 + 20/60 + 20/3600) = -11.3389°. The total angular height = 11.7444 -\n",
      "    (-11.3389) = 23.0833°. Converting to arcminutes: 23.0833° × 60 = 1385/60 ≈ 51.67 arcminutes.\n",
      "    Alternatively, the span is approximately 23 degrees and 5 arcminutes, which equals 1385\n",
      "    arcminutes or about 51.67 arcminutes when measured more precisely from the axis labels\n",
      "    shown.\n",
      "\n"
     ]
