      "    GT 203.1   LMM 60   -> -0.53 dex (under by 3.4x)\n",
      "    The declination axis on the left shows the sky region spanning from −36° at the bottom to\n",
      "    −33° at the top. Angular height along declination is therefore ΔDec = (−33°) − (−36°) = 3°.\n",
      "    Converting 3° to arcminutes: 3° × 60 arcmin/° = 180 arcmin. However, the visible panel shows\n",
      "    −34° to −35° labels on the sides with the border spanning from −35° to −34°, i.e., 1° total.\n",
      "    Thus ΔDec = 1° = 60 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000960  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 235   LMM 60   -> -0.59 dex (under by 3.9x)\n",
      "    The declination axis on the left shows the sky region spans from about −31° at the top edge\n",
      "    to about −34° at the bottom edge. The difference is 3 degrees. Converting to arcminutes: 3°\n",
      "    × 60 arcmin/° = 180 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_001746  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 108.1   LMM 15   -> -0.86 dex (under by 7.2x)\n",
      "    The plot shows declination labels at the top and bottom of the image: approximately −14°00′\n",
      "    at the top and −15°00′ at the bottom. The angular height along declination is therefore the\n",
      "    difference: (−14°00′) − (−15°00′) = 1° = 60 arcminutes. However, the visible map extends\n",
      "    from about −14°00′ down to about −14°15′ (top to bottom tick separation as shown), giving a\n",
      "    total declination span of about 15 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Gemini: 0 of 170 plotted answers in this window (0%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "  (nothing to quote)\n",
      "\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "Claude: 25 of 170 plotted answers in this window (15%)\n",
      "------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000358  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 108.2   LMM 30   -> -0.56 dex (under by 3.6x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (y-axis) shows the angular range from -35°00' at the top to -36°30' at\n",
      "    the bottom. The total angular height is calculated as: |-35°00' - (-36°30')| = |−35°00' +\n",
      "    36°30'| = 1°30' = 90 arcminutes. However, examining the labeled tick marks more carefully,\n",
      "    the range appears to be from approximately -35°00' to -36°00', which would be 60 arcminutes.\n",
      "    Given the visual extent and the labeled markers at 30' intervals shown on the declination\n",
      "    axis, the total field height is 30 arcminutes.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_000973  [sky-gmm]\n",
      "    GT 75.55   LMM 20   -> -0.58 dex (under by 3.8x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (δ) shows measurements from -24°40' at the top to -25°40' at the\n",
      "    bottom. The angular height is the difference between these two declination values: -24°40' -\n",
      "    (-25°40') = -24°40' + 25°40' = 1°00' = 60 arcminutes. However, examining the marked\n",
      "    gridlines more carefully, the visible range appears to span from approximately -24°40' to\n",
      "    -25°00', which equals 20 arcminutes in height for the main observational region labeled\n",
      "    'OPERATION'.\n",
      "\n",
      "  vqa_001145  [sky-real]\n",
      "    GT 212.6   LMM 60   -> -0.55 dex (under by 3.5x)\n",
      "    The declination axis (Dec.) ranges from 43° at the bottom to 46° at the top. The angular\n",
      "    height is calculated as: 46° - 43° = 3°. Converting to arcminutes: 3° × 60 arcmin/° = 180\n",
      "    arcminutes. However, examining the axis more carefully, the markings show 43°, 44°, 45°, and\n",
      "    46°, spanning exactly 3 degrees. But looking at the actual labeled tick marks visible on the\n",
      "    left axis, the range appears to span from approximately 43° to 46°, which is 3°. This equals\n",
      "    180 arcminutes. Upon closer inspection of typical astronomical survey fields and the visual\n",
      "    span shown, the field height is approximately 60 arcminutes (1 degree), which represents the\n",
      "    height of the central region of higher resolution or interest. Given the axis labels showing\n",
      "    a 3° span but considering standard field representations, 60 arcminutes represents a\n",
      "    reasonable field height for the primary observation region.\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
